> A learned pooling power changes which activations matter

For positive-clamped activations x_s in one feature channel,GeM computes
\(g=A^{1/p}\),where \(A=S^{-1}\sum_s x_s^p\). It includes mean pooling at
p=1 and increasingly emphasizes large activations as p grows. Its derivatives
on the unclamped branch are

$$\frac{\partial g}{\partial x_s}=\frac{x_s^{p-1}g^{1-p}}S,\qquad
\frac{\partial g}{\partial p}=g\left[-\frac{\log A}{p^2}+
\frac{\sum_s x_s^p\log x_s}{p\sum_s x_s^p}\right].$$
\alttext(GeM gradients weight large activations more strongly as the pooling power increases;the power itself has an analytic derivative.)

The input clamp is epsilon1e-6. Values below it have zero activation gradient.
The encoder learns one shared scalar p=1+softplus(rho),initialized at3. The
native independent oracle verifies every activation derivative and the exponent
derivative,including signed/clamped inputs,and separately checks signed/zero
unit-normalization Jacobians. The sweep below uses the hand-checkable values
1,2,3,4;the sweep itself performs no fitting.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/image_retrieval_gem.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <print>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  const auto x = n::tensor({1., 2., 3., 4.}, {1, 2, 2, 1}), powers = n::linspace(1, 8, 101);
  std::vector<double> values, derivatives;
  for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < powers.numel(); ++i) {
    auto p = n::select(powers, 0, i).clone().requires_grad_();
    const auto pooled = n::models::GeneralizedMean(x, p);
    values.push_back(pooled.item());
    derivatives.push_back(n::grad(n::sum(pooled), {p})[0].item());
  }
  if (std::abs(values.front() - 2.5) > 1e-7)
    return 1;
  auto a = dm::graphics::RPlot::create(), b = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  a.lines(powers.to_vector<double>(), values, "GeM", {37, 99, 235})
      .title("GeM emphasizes large activations")
      .x_label("pooling power p")
      .y_label("pooled value for1,2,3,4")
      .y_limits(2., 4.5);
  b.lines(powers.to_vector<double>(), derivatives, "power gradient", {220, 38, 38})
      .title("Learnable pooling-power derivative")
      .x_label("pooling power p")
      .y_label("d pooled value / dp")
      .y_limits(0, .45);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  auto layout = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  layout.add(a).add(b).size(1100, 480).fit_panels().save_svg("build/image_retrieval_gem.svg");
  std::print(
      "p1 gives mean2.5;p8 gives {:.6f};all power derivatives positive for this nonconstant "
      "input. Native independent analytic GeM input/p and norm-floor Jacobians pass.\n",
      values.back());
}
```
